WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:02.659
Film Courage: Why does a book fail to become
a movie?

00:00:02.659 --> 00:00:08.510
If someone wants to adapt a book, they buy
the rights, whatever it is, and it some how

00:00:08.510 --> 00:00:09.730
doesn’t translate, it doesn’t work out.

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:15.110
Dr. Ken Atchity, author/producer: Well, there
are hundreds of reasons why that can happen.

00:00:15.110 --> 00:00:20.380
But they come back in categories that you
get used to.

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:24.609
Every book that is submitted to Hollywood
is what’s called “covered.”

00:00:24.609 --> 00:00:31.750
And in my various webinars I talk about coverage
and coverage is an industry term for a story

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:39.510
report where a reader in the story department
of an agency or production company, a studio

00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:47.239
or any part of the business (where stories
go to be covered).

00:00:47.239 --> 00:00:52.160
And they’re covered because the executives
who make the decisions can’t read all the

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:58.329
stories that come in (too many things are
submitted).

00:00:58.329 --> 00:01:05.480
In the coverage it covers every part of the
story from a one-line pitch of the story to

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:11.610
the genre, to the length and category, the
quality of the writing, the dialogue, the

00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:17.710
characters, the supporting characters, the
main characters, supporting characters, plot,

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:18.710
etc.

00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:25.991
So you get a full report in four or five pages
that analyzes the story and it ends with a

00:01:25.991 --> 00:01:36.890
recommendation - pass, consider with development
or accept with development or just accept.

00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:39.880
Accepts are extremely rare.

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:45.050
Probably one to two percent are in that category.

00:01:45.050 --> 00:01:49.330
And the reason that most books are turned
down I’ve already mentioned some of them

00:01:49.330 --> 00:01:57.230
has to do with not clear who the protagonist
is, not strong enough antagonist, too many

00:01:57.230 --> 00:02:02.770
characters, you can’t figure out what’s
important, what’s not important, too much

00:02:02.770 --> 00:02:08.170
repetition, the dialogue (the characters don’t
sound different from each other) they all

00:02:08.170 --> 00:02:15.530
sound the same, and we all know from literature
graduate school that one of the common questions

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:20.730
that you ask is you’re just given lines
of dialogue from plays and asked to identify

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:24.240
the character from one line of dialogue.

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:30.960
The great playwrights make their dialogue
characteristic to each character and Lady

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:33.730
MacBeth would not be sounding like Juliet.

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:40.300
It will always be clear who is talking and
that is another reason for frequent turn downs

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:43.520
(the story isn’t big enough).

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:50.569
A story about Latvian Americans in a small
neighborhood in Detroit may get made as an

00:02:50.569 --> 00:02:58.130
indie movie if somebody like Merl Streep wants
to be in it because she is Latvian.

00:02:58.130 --> 00:03:04.480
But other than that the chances are that Fox
is not going to develop it because they are

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:08.760
looking at audience appeal, they are looking
at demographics.

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:16.850
So any of those reasons and all those reasons
are why a book gets turned down.

00:03:16.850 --> 00:03:22.131
Sometimes a book is too internal and screenwriters
struggle with it because they can’t figure

00:03:22.131 --> 00:03:28.650
out how to externalize the constant thinking
and philosophizing of the character.

00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:34.291
Examples of books that have done that well
like The World According To Garp is an example

00:03:34.291 --> 00:03:42.520
but they are usually internal stories are
very hard to turn into films.

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:46.130
What happens in half-way through your attempt
to do that you realize you’re inventing

00:03:46.130 --> 00:03:55.459
all the dialogue and therefore how true is
this movie to the book at all.

00:03:55.459 --> 00:04:00.630
Is it even the same book because if the book
did everything internally and you’re inventing

00:04:00.630 --> 00:04:03.500
all of the dialogue, you know what I mean?

00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:08.420
So there are a lot of reasons but they all
have to do with drama.

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:17.169
Drama is about scenes and a scene is a place
and time in which there is conflict, two forces

00:04:17.169 --> 00:04:21.769
come together in conflict and the conflict
is resolved.

00:04:21.769 --> 00:04:29.460
And that scene is the unit of drama and if
the scenes in a book are not clear enough,

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:34.520
scenes are very distinguished in books,Vonnegut
for example, his scenes can be two sentences

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:37.110
long.

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:45.130
In Faulkner his scenes can be 20 pages long
but still they will be clear scenes.

00:04:45.130 --> 00:04:52.520
My favorite example I think the shortest story
in American literature goes like this “Have

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:58.020
you lived next door to a man who is trying
to play the viola?

00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:01.319
She asked the police when she handed them
the empty revolver?”

00:05:01.319 --> 00:05:06.370
It’s a short story by Richard Broudagan.

00:05:06.370 --> 00:05:15.020
But there’s a whole scene, a whole story
told in a couple of lines just as tour-de-force

00:05:15.020 --> 00:05:21.879
to show you don’t need a lot of words to
make a scene (we get it right away).

00:05:21.879 --> 00:05:28.900
And drama is a scene like that and two components
of drama as I talk about in my various books

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:34.289
I mean one of them is action (she hands them
the empty revolver) and the other is dialogue

00:05:34.289 --> 00:05:40.350
(have you lived next door to a person learning
to play the viola?).

00:05:40.350 --> 00:05:47.210
Those are the two components of action and
drama (dialogue and action) and dialogue like

00:05:47.210 --> 00:05:52.690
“Good morning, how are you doing today?”
is not dramatic and yet many novels are filled

00:05:52.690 --> 00:05:55.289
with it (with that kind of dialogue).

00:05:55.289 --> 00:06:01.490
So the great novels that have been made into
great movies have vital dialogue that is really

00:06:01.490 --> 00:06:03.360
action dialogue.

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:08.499
A line from Hemingway that I love to quote
in creative writing classes these two people

00:06:08.499 --> 00:06:15.069
sitting near a train station and at one point
she says to him “Would you please, please,

00:06:15.069 --> 00:06:23.229
please, please, please, PLEASE stop taking?”

00:06:23.229 --> 00:06:27.620
And that’s a great example of a piece of
dialogue that is pure action.

00:06:27.620 --> 00:06:33.650
You know that there is no hope for their relationship
after she says that.

00:06:33.650 --> 00:06:37.849
And it goes on to say “The man did not say
anything for a moment and then asked would

00:06:37.849 --> 00:06:39.490
you like a beer?”

00:06:39.490 --> 00:06:42.719
And we know it’s all over between them.

00:06:42.719 --> 00:06:50.680
But there’s an example of how great dialogue
is you know from CHINATOWN “My mother, my

00:06:50.680 --> 00:06:51.680
sister.

00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:52.680
My mother, my sister.

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:53.899
My mother, my sister.”

00:06:53.899 --> 00:06:54.899
Remember that?

00:06:54.899 --> 00:06:56.240
“Tell the truth.”

00:06:56.240 --> 00:07:00.479
And she keeps saying the same thing over and
over again until he finally realizes that

00:07:00.479 --> 00:07:02.960
she is telling the truth.

00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:07.659
That’s when you know the writer knows what
he is doing.

00:07:07.659 --> 00:07:14.599
That’s why screenplay writing is so much
more difficult than novels because there are

00:07:14.599 --> 00:07:19.159
the harshest rules in writing screenplays
and a harsh rule is really only one harsh

00:07:19.159 --> 00:07:24.380
rule, every single word in the screenplay
is connected to every other word and in a

00:07:24.380 --> 00:07:26.370
novel that’s not true.

00:07:26.370 --> 00:07:33.419
I mean you can’t…in a 600-page novel it
can’t be true and it isn’t true but it

00:07:33.419 --> 00:07:39.550
is true in a screenplay because if you say
a word and the audiences leaves the theater

00:07:39.550 --> 00:07:44.669
and they loved it otherwise, you know they
are going to say “But why did he say that

00:07:44.669 --> 00:07:45.740
one thing to him?

00:07:45.740 --> 00:07:47.149
It made no sense?

00:07:47.149 --> 00:07:48.599
“Take care of yourself.”

00:07:48.599 --> 00:07:51.020
Why did he say that at the end of that scene?”

00:07:51.020 --> 00:07:54.600
And they won’t let go of that until they
have figured it out and if they can’t figure

00:07:54.600 --> 00:07:59.240
it out they go “There is something wrong
with that story.”

00:07:59.240 --> 00:08:05.439
You can’t focus the camera on a red hat
in a movie without making it pay off later.

00:08:05.439 --> 00:08:07.429
And that’s just not true in novels.

00:08:07.429 --> 00:08:14.610
One thing novels kind of float in the air
of the reader as you read the book you paint

00:08:14.610 --> 00:08:19.530
pictures in your head and movies are much
more demanding than that because they have

00:08:19.530 --> 00:08:23.789
to make decisions “What does she look like?”

00:08:23.789 --> 00:08:27.719
And you have to cast her with the right color
hair.

00:08:27.719 --> 00:08:33.949
And one of the most famous lines in history
is in The Iliad when everyone knows Helen

00:08:33.949 --> 00:08:37.640
of Troy is supposed to be the most beautiful
woman who ever lived, right?

00:08:37.640 --> 00:08:42.630
But Homer is not going to deal with that because
that’s just impossible.

00:08:42.630 --> 00:08:49.580
So what happens is when she appeared he says
the Elders of Troy were standing on the walls

00:08:49.580 --> 00:09:00.790
of Troy chattering like locust with each other
until a hush fell among them as Helen appeared.

00:09:00.790 --> 00:09:07.759
And one of them says “Terrible indeed is
her likeness to that of an immortal goddess.”

00:09:07.759 --> 00:09:15.709
And that is the entire description of Helen
of Troy which can’t be beat because it leaves

00:09:15.709 --> 00:09:19.550
completely to your imagination what she looked
like.

00:09:19.550 --> 00:09:26.040
He wasn’t about to say she was 5’2, red
hair, blue eyes, etc. which immediately will

00:09:26.040 --> 00:09:30.110
kill her beauty in some people’s minds.

00:09:30.110 --> 00:09:33.820
And so that’s why drama is so much more
challenging.

00:09:33.820 --> 00:09:41.080
It’s the ultimate expression of storytelling
that’s why movies are hugely powerful instruments

00:09:41.080 --> 00:09:41.920
around the world.

